	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44083</id><title>The Connection Between Urbanization and Poverty: How Cities Can Reduce Poverty – Or Deepen It</title><updated>2026-09-18T16:44:44-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44083" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/The-link-between_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/The-link-between_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/The-link-between_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/The-link-between_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, September 18 (IPS)  - Urbanization can help concentrate jobs, services, and investments; however, it can also concentrate unaffordable housing, insecure employment, and unequal exposure to contamination and environmental threats. A recent report from the UN &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.un.org/en/A/81/261&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Special Rapporteur on extreme poverty and human rights&lt;/a&gt;, argues that the difference is not urbanization itself, but how cities are planned, governed, and financed.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44083&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Connection Between Urbanization and Poverty: How Cities Can Reduce Poverty – Or Deepen It”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44081</id><title>Outpacing Foreign Aid, Migrant Remittances Are a Lifeline Ensuring Families Are Fed and Healthy, Says IFAD</title><updated>2026-09-18T08:11:47-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44081" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/jeong-yunji-XYDd48THgOU-unsplash.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/jeong-yunji-XYDd48THgOU-unsplash.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/jeong-yunji-XYDd48THgOU-unsplash-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/jeong-yunji-XYDd48THgOU-unsplash.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
BULAWAYO, Zimbabwe, September 18 (IPS)  - Migrants now send home more money than foreign aid, and this is pulling rural families out of poverty, according to the International Fund for Agricultural Development (IFAD).
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44081&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Outpacing Foreign Aid, Migrant Remittances Are a Lifeline Ensuring Families Are Fed and Healthy, Says IFAD”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44080</id><title>WHO Launches New Global Health Strategy for Childhood Cancer Medications</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:12:30-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44080" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/WHO-team-visits_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/WHO-team-visits_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/WHO-team-visits_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/WHO-team-visits_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, September 18 (IPS)  - When a child is diagnosed with cancer, their chances of survival should not depend on where they live, yet according to the WHO, more than 80 percent of children with cancer in high-income countries are cured, compared with fewer than 30 percent in many low- and middle-income countries (LMICs).
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44080&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “WHO Launches New Global Health Strategy for Childhood Cancer Medications”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44091</id><title>Security Council LIVE: UN peacekeeping under pressure</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44091" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;UN peacekeepers have increasingly been sent into places where there is no peace to keep – under resourced, outgunned and exposed to violence they are not equipped to counter. The Security Council meets this afternoon in New York to confront that problem, with Secretary-General António Guterres briefing ambassadors on how to close the gap.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44091&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Security Council LIVE: UN peacekeeping under pressure”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44090</id><title>‘Violence will only defer a problem’: UN Assembly President on trust, AI and lessons from the Rohingya crisis</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44090" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/02-06-2026-UN-Photo-SG-Rahman-Baerbock.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/02-06-2026-UN-Photo-SG-Rahman-Baerbock.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/02-06-2026-UN-Photo-SG-Rahman-Baerbock.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/02-06-2026-UN-Photo-SG-Rahman-Baerbock.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Khalilur Rahman took the gavel as President of the UN General Assembly&#039;s 81st session amid what he calls a crisis of confidence in the organization he has served – in one capacity or another – for 35 years. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44090&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Violence will only defer a problem’: UN Assembly President on trust, AI and lessons from the Rohingya crisis”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44089</id><title>Sexual violence against civilians and POW’s in Ukraine widespread and ‘abhorrent’</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44089" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/25-08-2026_Ukraine%20UNODC%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/25-08-2026_Ukraine%20UNODC%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/25-08-2026_Ukraine%20UNODC%202.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/25-08-2026_Ukraine%20UNODC%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Over 1,000 cases of sexual violence against civilians and prisoners of war (POWs) in Ukraine have been documented from the start of Russia’s invasion to the end of July this year.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44089&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Sexual violence against civilians and POW’s in Ukraine widespread and ‘abhorrent’”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44088</id><title>Less than 4 years from development goals deadline, progress shows what’s possible</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44088" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/18-09-2026_UN-Photo_SDG-Moment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/18-09-2026_UN-Photo_SDG-Moment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/18-09-2026_UN-Photo_SDG-Moment.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/18-09-2026_UN-Photo_SDG-Moment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For Benin, making progress on the UN’s Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) has meant finding new ways to finance development.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44088&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Less than 4 years from development goals deadline, progress shows what’s possible”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44087</id><title>Women and girls pay high price in DR Congo Ebola outbreak</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44087" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/29-07-2026-DRC-EBOLA-WHO-JUSTIN.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/29-07-2026-DRC-EBOLA-WHO-JUSTIN.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/29-07-2026-DRC-EBOLA-WHO-JUSTIN.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/29-07-2026-DRC-EBOLA-WHO-JUSTIN.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The deadly Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) has taken a disproportionate toll on women and girls, many of whom are on the frontlines caring for patients as well as their own families despite the high risk of infection.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44087&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Women and girls pay high price in DR Congo Ebola outbreak”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44086</id><title>Haiti: Child malnutrition crisis worsens amid escalating violence</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44086" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-08-2024-WFP-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-08-2024-WFP-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-08-2024-WFP-Haiti.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-08-2024-WFP-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Haiti is facing a worsening child malnutrition crisis, with an estimated 253,725 children aged 6 to 59 months projected to suffer from acute malnutrition between June 2026 and May 2027, new UN-backed analysis has revealed.   &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44086&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Haiti: Child malnutrition crisis worsens amid escalating violence”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44085</id><title>Nepal floods: ‘Apocalyptic’ destruction signals wider climate threat across Himalayas</title><updated>2026-09-18T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44085" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The catastrophic flooding that swept away entire villages in northern Nepal could occur again in the country or elsewhere in the Himalayas due to climate change.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/18/44085&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Nepal floods: ‘Apocalyptic’ destruction signals wider climate threat across Himalayas”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0005s ch:1 -->